  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1224 PM CDT Tue Oct 6 2026\n\nWeak mid level ridging and a slightly drier airmass across S TX will \nkeep rain chances very low to nil through the week. However, a broad \nmid level trough draped across the gulf waters and a broad surface \nlow across the Bay of Campeche will continue to bring a medium to \nhigh chance of showers and thunderstorms to the Middle Texas Coastal \nWaters through the end of the work week. But all of this is highly \ndependent on whether or not a tropical system develops across the \nBay of Campeche. The National Hurricane Center now has a 90% chance \nof development. If it does develop, it is expected to move north or \nnortheast, keeping the system far east of S TX. This typically \nresults in drier conditions on the west side of the system. Can not \nrule out an isolated morning or sea breeze shower each day. The \ndrier conditions may also lead to much warmer temperatures across S \nTX. \n\nA mid/upper level cut off low is progged to track/develop across \nWest TX which would provide upper support for convective development \nacross Mexico on Thu/Fri. This convection has a low to very low \nchance of moving across the Rio Grande and into western Webb County.\n\nThe main concern if a tropical system develops over the gulf will be \nan increased risk of coastal flooding, moderate to high rip current \nrisk and seas possibly building up to 9-10 feet. Currently a Coastal \nFlood Advisory is in effect through this evening due to a persistent \nnortheast flow across the northwest gulf waters pushing water up to \nthe dunes during times of high tide. Will not extend the advisory \nthrough tonight and not expecting coastal flooding on Wednesday as \nwinds across the gulf waters will be lighter. The last high tide \nearlier today reached just shy of 2 feet MSL and is expected to \nreach around 1.4-1.7 feet MSL with the next high tide. Additional \nCoastal Flood Advisories may be needed the latter part of the week \ndepending on tropical development in the gulf. Some models indicate \nswell periods may increase to 9-10 seconds by Friday along with seas \nbuilding to 9-10 feet or higher due to a strengthening northeast \nflow over the gulf waters. This will provide sufficient energy to\npush the water up to the dunes. If the winds strengthen to 25-30 \nknots with seas building to around 12 feet, then tide levels at \nhigh tide could reach 3 feet MSL and a Coastal Flood Warning would\nbe needed. However, chances are low that this will occur, but \nwill continue to monitor trends and possible tropical development.",
